now with the NHS coming under  unprecedented strain this winter nearly  half of parents say that they don't feel  confident about getting quick day-to-day  medical care for their children a Paul  by the website mums Ned also found that  a third were unable to get a GP  appointment for their child in the  previous three months well I'm joined  now by the chair of the Royal College of  gp's Professor Camilla Hawthorne very  good morning to you thanks so much for  talking to us first perhaps it's useful  for you to give us an idea of the kind  of pressures that GPS are under give us  a sense of what an average gp's working  day looks like  okay I think first of all I really  wanted to say how sad we are to see  these survey results because we want  parents to have confidence in the NHS  and in NHS services and certainly GPS  and their teams pull out all the stops  possible to see children  um to make sure they get timely and  appropriate care  so a GPS day nowadays is unbelievably  busy  um it's common for GPS to see between 40  and 60 patients in a day which is really  not sustainable and sometimes not very  safe I was talking to my brother-in-law  yesterday who was a GP in Farnam and by  tea time he'd already dealt with 140  patients which is way more than I've  ever heard before so it's a really  really busy pressurized atmosphere it  starts at about seven or eight in the  morning it can often go on till eight in  the evening GPS will then go home have a  quick bite to eat and then get onto  their laptops to do all the paperwork  that's accumulated as a result of seeing  all these patients so it is at the  moment we're working absolutely full  tilt now it isn't because GPS are not  seeing patients because they don't want  to they really do want to and it's a  source of enormous frustration for us to  feel that we're not able to do our job  in the way that we would like to and  that's because there just aren't enough  GPS due to years of underfunding  and poor workforce planning and in a way  the covert pandemic followed by strep a  followed by influenza is just  highlighting the nature of the problem  so GPS are feeling stretched according  to this poll a large number of people  feel that they struggle to get an  appointment for their children so what's  the solution  well the solution really is to have a  bigger Workforce you know we need at  least 6 000 more GPS that's what the  government Manifesto said in 2019 to be  delivered by 2024 so they're way behind  on that because in fact the numbers of  GPS are actually dropping so we're now  about 700 short of where we were two  years ago we're not increasing in  numbers and a lot of that is due to  burnout and people leaving the  profession early and what we really need  is better retention strategies for GPS  to keep people in the job and working  seeing patients in the way that they  would like to do  well yes and in fact in the mum's net  poll 86 said that they blamed the  government for the crisis in the NHS do  you agree then  uh yes I think that it's been a number  of factors really  um you know you can't blame the  government for covid-19 or for strep a  but those have really just highlighted  the underlying uh shortage in Workforce  and we've been uh warning the government  about this for at least a decade  probably longer that numbers of GPS were  um were falling and that this was going  to become a crisis and also that in  order to run a functioning Health  Service you need a robust general  practice you need to have a good Primary  Care Service and there's very good  evidence from across the world that  countries that have a good functioning  Primary Care Service have a much  healthier population  we're seeing walkouts by nurses and  ambulance staff is there a mood amongst  GPS for industrial action do GPS want to  strike  no I don't think they do at the moment  um it's not really our function as a  Royal Medical College to comment on  terms and conditions of um of of work so  it's not really within our remit to  comment on this and we wouldn't normally  discuss it but um speaking anecdotally  I've not heard of a concerted move  amongst GPS wanting to go on strike at  the minute  however beyond the strikes amongst  nurses and ambulance workers do you  think it's likely that we could see more  widespread walkouts amongst other  healthcare workers  I think there is just so much pressure  um on Healthcare staff right across the  NHS and it's really important that Staff  feel able to do their job properly to  the standards that they're trained for  and under the right conditions and that  is really I think what is underlying a  lot of the dissent and discontent that  we have currently  and and I wonder if you have have a view  on the ongoing dispute with the nurses  uh we have seen reported this morning by  the Times newspaper that uh Pat Cullen  the the head of the RCN the the nurses  Union at saying that she might be  prepared to meet the government halfway  would look at an offer of around 10 even  though the nurses Union has asked for 19  originally what do you make of that and  do you feel that progress could be made  I really hope so I think no NHS worker  wants to strike it doesn't matter who or  what you are within the NHS resolution  is the best thing for everybody  concerned and of course particularly for  our patients it's not for us to get into  details of what this might look like I'm  afraid  um but I think whatever happens it's  absolutely essential that we all work  together to minimize the impact on  patient safety  well Professor Camilla Hawthorne a chair  of the Royal College of GPS we really  appreciate your time this morning thanks  so much for talking to us thank you
 

